WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.450
- What is the one thing about mixing

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.613
that people don't understand?

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.423
(upbeat music)

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.210
And in this particular film, the dialogue,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.590
were two major issues.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.720
One is that everybody's had to decide

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.440
what kind of accent we should have-

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.230
- Mm-hmm.
- And I decided

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.690
that everybody should be
speaking American English

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.870
with an Italian accent, as if
they were native born Italians

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.460
in America and had learned English.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.400
But it also brings with it a
question of intelligibility.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.790
I decided that place
names should be in Italian

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.310
and proper nouns should be in
Italian and spoken in Italian.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.560
- And this outside capital
will magically bestow

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.210
its favors upon Maserati.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:47.190
- What did that pose for you
in terms of intelligibility

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.130
and how the dialogue tracks came in?

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.120
- When we were working together,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.860
you were very aware of cadences,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.850
of how an Italian star
would drop off more-

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:56.683
- Right, yeah.

00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:57.516
- At the end of a sentence and things.

00:00:57.516 --> 00:00:59.550
So we did quite a lot
of volume manipulation

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.710
with certain syllables
as well at the ends too.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.360
- And you really are
talking about syllables

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.800
is because we would drop-
- Yes.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.230
- Just the very end of
a syllable of a word.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.710
I recall that we found that
sometimes shortening a vowel,

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.600
if an accent wasn't quite right,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.250
particularly on an American actor,

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.220
speaking Italian accent
in American English,

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.170
if the accent, it wasn't right,

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.480
it was typically because
they were drawing out

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.313
like an A-
- Yes.

00:01:22.313 --> 00:01:23.460
- [Michael] Of this word Chicago.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.293
Chicago.

00:01:24.293 --> 00:01:27.900
- When you and I first worked
together many years ago now,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.603
we could never do that on film because-

00:01:29.603 --> 00:01:30.930
- I know.
- We couldn't get in

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.670
that close on the magnetic tape.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.870
- It was our first film, Heat.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.910
- [Andy] I came in right at
the very end of Mohicans,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:36.743
but Heat was the one-
- Heat!

00:01:36.743 --> 00:01:38.790
- We first worked on together, yeah.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:39.780
Thinking of motor itself.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.300
one of the fun scenes that
I enjoyed doing the most

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.120
for the sound was actually
the Maserati time trial.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.070
I can't remember if it was
fully in the script like that,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.290
where we were cutting backwards
and forwards to the church

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.510
with the Mozart going
back onto the Maserati.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.090
The beauty of that with
the rawness of the engine,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.370
the gorgeous vocal, the gorgeous singing.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:57.203
And then, of course,

00:01:57.203 --> 00:01:59.280
we introduced all the
fun of the stopwatches.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.410
They were at church, but
really they were listening to,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:02.700
they heard the gun go off

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.440
for the beginning of the time trial.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.273
- [Michael] Right.

00:02:05.273 --> 00:02:06.106
- And they all switched
their watches on 'em,

00:02:06.106 --> 00:02:08.250
and that was such a great
sort of juxtaposition

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.300
of all those different sounds.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.490
It was definitely one of
the most fun things to do.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.210
The opera scene was also a
very special moment I thought

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.400
for sound because it was a little opposite

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.570
of what we would normally do.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.270
We started in the opera house,
we hear the distant singing,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.910
which Laura's hearing 'cause
she hasn't gone to the opera,

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.735
now (indistinct) is hearing
and she's not there.

00:02:25.735 --> 00:02:28.406
(opera music)

00:02:28.406 --> 00:02:30.720
We cut to the opera house,
we hear it in its beauty,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.790
and by the way, they were
recorded live singing.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:33.623
- [Michael] Right.

00:02:33.623 --> 00:02:36.126
- So that was a challenge,
but again, very authentic.

00:02:36.126 --> 00:02:38.626
(opera music)

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.940
Then we go on stage with
them and we dried it all out.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.530
We took the reverbs down a lot

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.540
so we weren't suddenly in
the opera house anymore.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.830
We became more intimate.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.420
And I think that was an interesting choice

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.770
that you asked me to do-
- Yeah.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.240
- But it worked so beautifully

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.400
because now suddenly
you've lost a performance

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.560
and you've gone into what
they're all thinking.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.457
- Right.
- As you cut to each person.

00:02:57.457 --> 00:02:59.580
(opera music)

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.260
As you see their eyes-
- Well, the design, yeah.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.340
- [Andy] You read something into that.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.990
- My design for that scene
was to invert the performance.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.500
I wanted opera singers lit
as if they were the subjects

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.460
of the scene.
- Yes.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.600
- And not the actors.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.370
Yeah, and the camera
work is very intimate,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.500
it's very verite right in their faces,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.827
as if the two characters
we're the most familiar with,

00:03:19.827 --> 00:03:24.210
the tenor and the soprano,
singing the aria from traviata.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.170
Then the audience, our characters,
Enzo Ferrari, Lina Lardy,

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.270
Shailene Woodley, what opera does to you,

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.180
it invokes memory, and
then what's most alive

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.490
is their flashbacks and
what they're recalling.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.550
And each one, it became an opportunity

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.750
to explore the backstory
in totally emotional terms

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:47.430
of young Enzo Ferrari, young
Laura when Dino was three,

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.320
four years old, and Enzo's dancing

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.880
with the dress in the 1930s,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.580
and he's singing part of the aria.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.580
- Mmm, the aria.
- So it's like a three-way-

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.660
- Yeah.
- Perspective,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.610
all of which is then cut through

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.925
with the savage red car racing at you.

00:04:02.925 --> 00:04:03.758
- The French Grand Prix.

00:04:03.758 --> 00:04:05.405
- Very abrupt cut to
the French Grand Prix.

00:04:05.405 --> 00:04:08.220
(car revving)

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.670
In this film, you had to deal
with the sound of the cars,

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.740
and it is music.
- Absolutely.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.950
- But the sound of the
cars, the authentic sound

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.900
of not regular, commercial B12s,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.400
but full competition B12
Ferrari engines, is powerful,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.640
it's beautiful, it's scary, it's inviting,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.587
it's all these things
all at the same time.

00:04:28.587 --> 00:04:31.087
(car revving)

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.880
We had done a lot of temp with
various different car sounds

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.870
when we were editing.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.120
We always, with the
intention of going back

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.667
and then recording-
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.330
- The real thing.
- Yeah, the real thing.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.860
- Which we eventually did.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.480
Do you wanna talk about how to,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.027
how we managed the extreme dynamics?

00:04:49.027 --> 00:04:51.750
- (chuckles) So obviously,
you had to create cars

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.390
that you could put cameras in
and would go at a good speed.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.760
You got 'em up to a hundred,
120 miles an hour, those-

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.500
- We would do 130, 140.
- Yep.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.660
But obviously, they didn't
have quite that sound.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.940
So we were able to access the vehicles,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.480
put them out into a little
race trap in England,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.310
and record just sound only.
- Right.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.040
- So that's how we created the
sound of the real vehicles.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.500
As far as the dynamics
go, a lot of that is down

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.110
to the way that the script
and the way you shot it,

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.520
the way it was cut.
- Right.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.010
- Because it had these
hard sort of juxtapositions

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.510
all the time, which was so great.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.910
You'd get a very intense dialogue scene,

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.190
which allowed us to strip backgrounds

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.220
and things out of and become
very almost claustrophobic-

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.090
- Right.
- In the drama-

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.620
- Yeah.
- Between our characters.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.880
And then you, on a cut,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.547
we'd go into full-blooded roar of engines.

00:05:35.547 --> 00:05:37.680
(car skidding and revving)

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.390
I mean, that, from a sound point of view,

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.370
was actually a lot of fun to do.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.980
When you think about music,
that's also a big component

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.380
because how can music be more exciting

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.750
than the sound of those real engines?

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:51.690
Is it, tackling a period piece like this,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.000
is it hard for the
specificity of the period

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:54.930
and the location?

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.620
- My ambition is to bring
audience into the world.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.137
The amount of detail and everything

00:06:00.137 --> 00:06:01.860
that I would wanna bring into it

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.320
to make it complete is vast.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:05.700
This is where everything really happens.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:06.826
- Right.

00:06:06.826 --> 00:06:07.659
- So you pick up a residence

00:06:07.659 --> 00:06:09.510
off walking through the real place,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.910
but it's completely unchanged.
- Really?

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.730
- And there's nothing built
after the 19th century

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.250
in the whole of three-fifths of motordom

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.083
- [Andy] Oh.

00:06:18.083 --> 00:06:20.040
- So everywhere you look,
the cobblestone streets,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.570
everything is totally period.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.460
There's a lot of signage
you had to take off.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.970
The Ferrari house is
actually Piero's house,

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.790
and if we walk around the
corner from the Ferrari house,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.190
there's a barbershop where
he gets shaved every morning.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.250
So Adam sat in a chair that Enzo sat in

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.980
and was shaved in the film
by the son of the barber-

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:39.772
- On the set, oh my god.
- Who shaved Enzo.

00:06:39.772 --> 00:06:40.605
- [Andy] That's fantastic.

00:06:40.605 --> 00:06:42.300
- So there was so much of that resonance.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.150
In mixing, it's the final writing,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.070
it's the final authoring of the movie,

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.100
and you have every
component, it's not a script,

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.680
which is a blueprint of what
you want to have happen,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.500
and it's not the shooting,
which is so fragmented.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.440
When you're mixing, it
is all there for real.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.273
- Right.
- Okay?

00:06:59.273 --> 00:07:00.360
It's all around you.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.750
What is the one thing about mixing

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.630
that people don't understand?

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.360
- If you notice something,
then it's probably not right.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.300
What I try and aim to do
is make you not notice

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.770
that we're doing anything particularly

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.240
with the soundtrack, you know?

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.060
Because if you start to hear
things that confuse you,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.080
then you've lost people.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.640
Clarity is number one to me,
it's always been the same way.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:22.473
- Right.

00:07:22.473 --> 00:07:23.940
- I've mixed in a very
traditional style, as you know.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.780
It's about making sure that
it's very clear, the storyline,

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.550
the narrative, the atmospheres

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.740
are very important around that.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.290
And then of course, the
music, the music choices,

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.150
where the music comes
in, where it goes out.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:36.983
- Right.

00:07:36.983 --> 00:07:37.816
- They're all signals

00:07:37.816 --> 00:07:39.990
that you're supposed to pay
attention to subconsciously.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.310
If it's in the wrong place,

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:42.960
you notice it for the wrong reasons.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:43.793
- Right.

00:07:43.793 --> 00:07:45.240
- So what I try and do every day

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.120
is make something as
seamless and as meant to be

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.830
as it can be for that story.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.200
- [Michael] It's the flow of
the narrative through times.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.033
- Absolutely.

00:07:53.033 --> 00:07:56.070
- And the rhythm of that
flow from quiet scenes

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.100
to loud scenes to when
you have the juxtaposition

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.020
of a dynamic sound coming on.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.268
That's what's particularly exciting.

00:08:03.268 --> 00:08:04.101
- [Andy] It is exciting.

00:08:04.101 --> 00:08:07.440
- That I rely on you very much
to be able to calm me down

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.150
and keep it in a-
- I do my best.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.060
And often, you and I will sit
and we'll go through something

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.830
and then you'll go back
and make an adjustment

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.450
in the editing room with the picture.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.283
- Right.

00:08:16.283 --> 00:08:18.450
- Because it only becomes
apparent while we're mixing sound

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.160
that the picture needs
to have an adjustment.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.122
- [Michael] When we mix,

00:08:21.122 --> 00:08:22.710
I move the editing rooms
on the mixing stage,

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.680
basically an adjacent
room, so that we can make-

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:26.820
- Yeah, constant adjustments.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.020
- Because it really is totally-

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:28.853
- Yeah.

00:08:28.853 --> 00:08:29.686
- It's interactive and organic.

00:08:29.686 --> 00:08:30.519
- [Andy] It's very interactive.

00:08:30.519 --> 00:08:32.013
- It's very much alive.
- Yep.

00:08:32.013 --> 00:08:34.650
- It's the best of times
when I get to park the car

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:36.631
in front of the sound stage and go in

00:08:36.631 --> 00:08:38.760
and I'm there every minute.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:39.660
- Yes, you are.
- With you.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:40.493
(chuckling)

00:08:40.493 --> 00:08:41.326
And it's really, it's a real pleasure.

00:08:41.326 --> 00:08:42.557
I keep, I always look forward to it.

00:08:42.557 --> 00:08:43.822
- Great, well, me too, thank you.

00:08:43.822 --> 00:08:44.655
- I think that's a good endpoint.

00:08:44.655 --> 00:08:46.050
- Thank you, it's been
great talking to you.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:46.883
Thank you so much.
- Good.

00:08:46.883 --> 00:08:47.891
Yeah, you're welcome.

00:08:47.891 --> 00:08:50.474
(upbeat music)

